e bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': "**Analyzing the River Decision**\n\nOkay, here's the situation. It's heads-up Texas Hold'em, and we're on the river. I've got a pair of nines, but the board is 5-5-9-Q-K. That means I have two pair, nines and fives, with a king kicker. Not great, but not terrible. The preflop and the flop were checked down. Now the river is where the action is.\n\nI'm out of position, and I'm first to act. The pot is likely small, since it was a limp pot. Pre-flop I was the small blind and called.\n\nMy opponent's actions have been passive throughout the hand - check, check, check. Given that runout, it seems like they probably don't have a monster hand, or they would have bet for value. But the Queen and King that came on the turn and river could have hit their range. They could have a Queen or King, and that beats me, and they could also have a straight, J-10. So I'm beat by a lot of hands.\n\nIf I bet, I'm only going to get called by better hands, like the K or Q that beats me. I am unlikely to get value from a worse hand, unless the opponent is really, really stubborn. If I check, the opponent might bluff, or I might be able to get to showdown if they have a worse hand, which is not improbable. I beat Ace high and smaller pairs.\n\nWe haven't been aggressive at all. The pot is small, and with the Queen and King on the board, it's hard to extract value, and the river cards are scary. The best play is to check it down and hope to get to the end for free, and possibly induce a bluff. It is the best way to not lose more chips, and to take what I can if I am ahead.\n\nTherefore, I'm going to check.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: I hold a pair of 9s, giving me Two Pair (Nines and Fives) with a King kicker. The board runout contains two overcards (Queen and King) to my pair. The action has been passive (check-check) on all previous streets (Pre-flop, Flop, Turn), indicating that the opponent likely does not hold a strong hand like a 5 or a set of 9s. However, the River King and Turn Queen connect well with random broadway hands or a straight draw (J-10). If I bet, I am likely only called by hands that beat me (K-x, Q-x) and will fold out hands I currently beat (Ace-high, lower pairs). Checking is the optimal play to realize my showdown value without bloating the pot against a potentially superior rivered hand, or to induce a bluff from a missed draw.\n\nReason: My hand has moderate showdown value but is vulnerable to the overcards (Q, K) on the board. Betting yields little value as worse hands fold and better hands call. Checking allows me to see the showdown cheaply.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
