 No gain. But I want to win.\nCall (0)? High variance. Potential gain.\nRaise? High variance. I have no strong hand, so this is a bluff.\n\nThe best answer is to find out the other play. If I *lose* because I make a bad call, I learn.\n\nIf I win, I *win*.\n\nOkay, I choose to call, because the potential payoff is too attractive to pass up. Now I need to wait to see what the cards are. Let\'s see if this decision was correct or if I should have folded.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: You hold Two Pair (Queens and Threes) with a Ten kicker on a board of 8c, Qs, 3c, 6d, Qh. The opponent has led betting on the Flop, Turn, and River with small amounts. Your hand is a "bluff catcher"—it beats missed draws (like club flush draws or straight draws) but loses to any Queen, 8, 6, or pocket pair higher than 3s.\n\nReason: The opponent\'s consistent betting suggests they likely have a piece of the board (an 8, 6, or Q), which would beat your pair of 3s. However, the bet size (119 chips) is very small relative to the pot, offering excellent pot odds (better than 4:1). You only need to win approximately 20% of the time to justify a call. Given the small cost and the possibility of the opponent bluffing with missed draws, calling is the most mathematically sound decision despite the weakness of your hand.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
